Law text: only words out of copyright.

Copyright Act 1991 (AT 8 of 1991), section 157 (Copyright in Acts and Measures), as published on legislation.gov.im:

  "(1) Her Majesty is entitled to copyright in every Act of Tynwald, Act of Parliament or Measure of the
  General Synod of the Church of England.
  (2) The copyright subsists from Royal Assent until the end of the period of 50 years from the end of the
  calendar year in which Royal Assent was given.
  ...
  (5) No other copyright, or right in the nature of copyright, subsists in an Act of Tynwald, Act of
  Parliament or Measure."

So the copyright in an Act of Tynwald that received Royal Assent in 1975 or earlier has ended. This
repository holds text only from such Acts, and only those provisions of them whose endnotes name no
amending Act or other instrument made after 1975 (words put in by a later instrument may still be in
copyright) and that show no fine amounts (the Acts' general notes say fines were raised by later Acts).
The text is taken from the official revised editions published by the Isle of Man Government
(Attorney General's Chambers) at https://legislation.gov.im. The editorial notes, endnotes and
typographical arrangement of those editions are not copied. Acts assented after 1975 remain Crown
copyright and are not copied here.

Catalogue entries (short title, citation, Royal Assent date) are facts taken from the Acts' own front
matter and from Wikipedia's "List of acts of Tynwald" (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_acts_of_Tynwald).
Facts are not copyrightable; the list's own prose, which is licensed CC BY-SA 4.0, is not reproduced.

The "legal-system" facts come from Wikidata (https://www.wikidata.org), released under CC0 1.0.

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the
tools) are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal: https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
